.Dd November 11, 2026
.Dt WRITE 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm write ,
.Nm writev ,
.Nm pwrite ,
.Nm pwrite64 ,
.Nm pwritev
.Nd write bytes to a descriptor
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In unistd.h
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn write "int fd" "const void *buf" "size_t count"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn pwrite "int fd" "const void *buf" "size_t count" "off_t offset"
.In sys/uio.h
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn writev "int fd" "const struct iovec *iov" "int iovcnt"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn pwritev "int fd" "const struct iovec *iov" "int iovcnt" "off_t offset"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn write
writes up to
.Fa count
bytes from
.Fa buf
to
.Fa fd .
A successful call may write fewer bytes than requested; a count past
0x7ffff000, Linux's
.Dv MAX_RW_COUNT ,
writes at most that many, so that the count returned is never negative.
The descriptor is checked first, then the range with the original count:
an overflowing range or one past the top of user memory is
.Er EFAULT ,
even for a count of zero.
On the i686 kernel, a count negative as the kernel's signed size is
.Er EINVAL
after that range check, before the transfer is limited.
An i386 call on x86-64 uses the kernel's 64-bit signed size for this check.
The operation depends on the descriptor's backing object.
.Ss Regular files
A regular file is written through its mounted file system at the
open file description's offset.
A positive result advances that offset by the number of bytes written.
Duplicated descriptors and descriptors inherited through fork share
the offset and status flags.
With
.Dv O_APPEND ,
each write uses the current end of the file, regardless of an intervening seek.
.Pp
A write to a file on a read-only mount fails with
.Er EROFS .
A file attached to a loop device cannot be written directly;
the call fails with
.Er EBUSY .
A write of one or more bytes by an unprivileged process clears setuid and,
when group execute is set, setgid.
.Pp
On ext2, allocated overwrites and contiguous appends can remain in a
bounded mount-wide batch after a successful write.
Reads and stat see accepted bytes and size immediately.
The batch drains at fsync, sync, a bound, an attribute or namespace change,
or the last close of an open file description.
Sparse writes and hole fills drain before returning.
An I/O error after some bytes were accepted returns their count;
fsync reports the delayed error even after close and reopen.
.Ss Pipes and terminals
A pipe write waits when the pipe is full.
With
.Dv O_NONBLOCK ,
it returns the bytes already written, or
.Er EAGAIN
if none fit.
When no reader remains, the calling thread receives
.Dv SIGPIPE ;
the call returns the bytes already written, or
.Er EPIPE
if none were written.
.Pp
Console output passes through the terminal output processor to both
the screen and serial port.
With
.Dv OPOST
and
.Dv ONLCR
set, a newline becomes carriage return followed by newline.
The return value counts input bytes, before this conversion.
Console writes give waiting processors and queued threads a turn after
each block of at most 256 input bytes; one block and its serial waits
still hold the kernel lock.
Pseudoterminal slave output uses the same processing and enters the
master's 4096-byte ring.
A slave writer waits for room, or returns a short count or
.Er EAGAIN
when nonblocking; echo that arrives while the ring is full is discarded.
One slave write reaches the ring before another begins, as
.Xr console 4
describes.
Writing a slave whose master has closed fails with
.Er EIO .
Master writes supply input to the slave's line discipline and may wait
for input space, or fail with
.Er EAGAIN
when nonblocking.
.Pp
A background process writing its controlling terminal receives
.Dv SIGTTOU
when
.Dv TOSTOP
is set: the write posts the signal to its process group and returns
.Er EINTR .
If the signal is blocked or ignored, the write goes ahead.
Otherwise, in an orphaned process group, it fails with
.Er EIO ,
as on Linux.
.Ss Sockets
A stream socket queues bytes for TCP transmission.
If no send space is available, it waits unless
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
is set.
A short return counts only the bytes queued.
A connection no longer able to send returns its recorded error, or
.Er EPIPE
when no error was recorded.
This write path does not generate
.Dv SIGPIPE
for a socket.
.Pp
A datagram socket must have a connected peer.
Each write sends one datagram, with at most 65507 payload bytes over IPv4
or 65527 over IPv6.
A broadcast destination requires
.Dv SO_BROADCAST .
Other socket types return
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
.Ss Devices
Devices with a write operation use that operation.
Whole-disk and partition nodes return
.Er EPERM
on the ordinary kernel; their recovery-kernel writes and writes through
the bounded boot devices require root and sector alignment.
See
.Xr disk 4 .
.Pp
A write of the framebuffer,
.Pa /fb ,
copies bytes to the pixels of the mode in force at the descriptor's
offset, counted from the top left pixel in rows of the pitch
.Dv FB_GETINFO
reports; see
.Xr ioctl 2 .
A write that would pass the mode's end, 64000 bytes in mode 13h and the
pitch times the height in the native mode, stops at it.
In the native mode the offset moves past what was written, so
.Xr lseek 2
and
.Fn write
place rows as
.Fn pwrite
does; in mode 13h it stays, so that each write from 0 is a whole frame.
Only the process that owns the display writes it.
.Pp
A write of
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /mem
or
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /oom_score_adj
is the file's own, as
.Xr proc 5
describes it, and holds the open file description for its length, as
.Xr lseek 2
holds it to move the offset.
.Ss Gather writes
.Fn writev
writes the buffers described by
.Fa iov
in order:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct iovec {
        void *iov_base;
        size_t iov_len;
};
.Ed
.Pp
The descriptor is checked first: one not open, opened with
.Dv O_PATH
or not open for writing gives
.Er EBADF ,
and one open for writing on an object with no write method, such as an
epoll set,
.Er EINVAL ,
before the count and the vector are looked at, as on Linux.
The vector count must be between 0 and 1024.
Zero-length entries are skipped.
Each nonempty entry is passed to the same operation as
.Fn write .
The call stops at the first short write or error.
Before a byte is written the kernel checks each length, then that each
range lies in user space, as Linux's import_iovec does: a length negative
as a signed size, which for a 32-bit program is one past 2147483647,
gives
.Er EINVAL ,
and a range outside user space
.Er EFAULT .
The total is cut at 0x7ffff000, as one write's count is, and the buffer
that crosses it is written only up to it.
.Pp
For an append to a mounted file, the kernel copies the whole vector, then
brings in its buffers before taking the file lock.
A bad data page ends the accessible prefix; bytes before it are written
and returned as a short count.
A pseudo-terminal master also copies the whole vector, and holds its
write lock across every element, including waits and user copies, so
another writer cannot insert bytes between them.
Other writes read each vector again as they reach it.
An error found after a positive transfer returns the transferred byte
count instead of the error.
With zero vectors, or a readable vector list of zero-length entries
only,
.Fn writev
returns zero once the descriptor is checked, without the write itself:
.Pa /dev/full
and an unconnected datagram socket return zero, not
.Er ENOSPC
or
.Er EDESTADDRREQ .
An entry that cannot be read gives
.Er EFAULT .
.Pp
An append to a mounted file holds its write lock across the vector.
Other vectors are a sequence of separate writes.
On a datagram socket, each nonempty entry sends a separate datagram.
Musl's buffered output uses
.Fn writev .
.Ss Positioned writes
.Fn pwrite
writes up to
.Fa count
bytes at
.Fa offset
of the file
.Fa fd
is open on and leaves the descriptor's offset where it was.
Its checks are those of
.Xr pread 2 ,
in the same order, with a descriptor not open for writing returning
.Er EBADF ;
a directory is never open for writing, so it returns
.Er EBADF
as well.
With
.Dv O_APPEND ,
a regular file is written at its end whatever
.Fa offset
says, as Linux does and POSIX does not.
The regular-file rules of
.Fn write
apply otherwise: a file backing a loop device returns
.Er EBUSY ,
and a write of one or more bytes by an unprivileged process clears the
set-id bits.
A raw disk window is written at
.Fa offset ,
and so is the framebuffer.
The offset the descriptor shares with its duplicates does not change at
any point of the call.
.Pp
The i386 system call numbers are 4 for
.Nm write ,
146 for
.Nm writev ,
181 for
.Nm pwrite64 ,
which takes the descriptor, the buffer and the count in EBX, ECX and
EDX, and the offset's low and high 32 bits in ESI and EDI,
334 for
.Nm pwritev ,
whose offset is in the fourth and fifth arguments, low word first, and
379 for
.Nm pwritev2 ,
which adds flags in the sixth.
Musl's and GNU libc's
.Fn pwrite
are
.Nm pwrite64 .
.Pp
On x86-64 the numbers are 1 for
.Nm write ,
20 for
.Nm writev ,
18 for
.Nm pwrite64 ,
which takes the offset whole in R10, 296 for
.Nm pwritev
and 328 for
.Nm pwritev2 ,
each with the offset whole in its fourth argument and its fifth, the
offset's high half, not read.
.Fn pwritev
writes the buffers of
.Fa iov
as
.Fn pwrite
writes one, each at the place the ones before it reached, from
.Fa offset ;
the offset and the descriptor are checked first, as
.Fn pwrite
checks them, then the vector whole before a byte is written, and a short
write ends the call.
On an O_APPEND file the whole vector goes to the end under one lock,
leaving the shared offset alone, and a bad data page returns the bytes
written before it, as an append through writev does.
.Pp
The x86-64 kernel runs, for a program that enters it by
.Ql int $0x80 ,
which
.Xr ia32 7
describes, i386's
.Nm writev
(146), whose vector is of 32-bit addresses and lengths, and i386's
.Nm pwritev
(334) and
.Nm pwritev2
(379), with i386's arguments.
.Pp
.Nm pwritev2
writes as
.Nm writev
at offset -1 and as
.Nm pwritev
at another, and after a write with
.Dv RWF_DSYNC
or
.Dv RWF_SYNC
does what
.Fn fdatasync
or
.Fn fsync
does, to the file the write went to even when another thread closes the
descriptor meanwhile.
.Dv RWF_APPEND
writes at the file's end, moving the descriptor's offset only when the
supplied offset is -1, and fails with the file system's error when the
end cannot be read.
.Dv RWF_NOAPPEND
ignores the description's
.Dv O_APPEND
for this call: an explicit offset is used without moving the shared offset,
and -1 uses and advances the shared offset.
The description keeps
.Dv O_APPEND
for other calls.
On an append-only file it fails with
.Er EPERM .
Combining
.Dv RWF_APPEND
and
.Dv RWF_NOAPPEND
is
.Er EINVAL
for a nonempty vector.
An append, by
.Dv RWF_APPEND
or by
.Dv O_APPEND ,
reads the end and writes there under one hold of the file's lock, or, on
a file system with no lock of each file, such as FAT32 and exFAT, of the
mount's lock, so that two opens of one file appending at once each add
their bytes.
A vector append holds that lock across all its elements;
another open's append cannot split a record between elements.
.Dv RWF_HIPRI
is also accepted.
The descriptor is checked first, then the vector is imported, as Linux does:
an unreadable list is
.Er EFAULT ,
an excessive count or a negative signed length is
.Er EINVAL ,
and a vector of no bytes returns zero before flags are checked.
Only then is another flag
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
The data pages are touched after the flags have been accepted.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
On success, these functions return the number of bytes written.
.Fn writev
returns the total across the buffers.
On failure they return -1 and set
.Va errno .
The raw kernel calls return a negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/errnoprobe.c
issues system call 146 with standard output as the descriptor,
.Ql 0xFFFFF000
as the vector address, and a vector count of 1.
It requires
.Er EFAULT .
.Pa tests/musl/preadmusl.c
writes a file at offsets with
.Fn pwrite ,
checks that the descriptor's offset does not move, and that
.Dv O_APPEND
writes at the end.
It writes buffers of 2, 0 and 5 bytes at offset 12 with
.Fn pwritev ,
requires 7, and requires
.Er EFAULT
and no byte written for a vector whose second buffer is a kernel
address.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width ECONNREFUSED
.It Er EACCES
A datagram broadcast was attempted without
.Dv SO_BROADCAST ,
or a loop-control attachment path has a directory the caller cannot
search.
.It Er EAGAIN
A nonblocking pipe, pseudoterminal master, or stream socket cannot
accept bytes without waiting, or a datagram socket cannot acquire
an ephemeral port.
.It Er EBADF
The descriptor is invalid, is not open for writing, is of a kind with no
write, or was opened with
.Dv O_PATH .
.It Er EBUSY
A regular file backs a loop device, or a loop-control command encounters
an occupied device, an attached file, a mount, a held buffer, or a transfer.
A framebuffer write comes from a process that does not own the display,
such as a child that inherited the descriptor, or the display left its
mode before the first byte.
.It Er ECONNREFUSED
A stream connection was refused.
.It Er ECONNRESET
A stream connection was reset.
.It Er EDESTADDRREQ
A datagram socket has no connected peer.
.It Er EFAULT
The original buffer range passes the top of user memory, or the buffer
or vector list cannot be read as required.
.It Er EFBIG
A file write exceeds the file system's size or addressing limit, or a
framebuffer write starts past the mode's end.
.It Er EINTR
A signal interrupted a wait, or a background terminal write posted
.Dv SIGTTOU .
.It Er EINVAL
The descriptor is open for writing on an object with no write method,
such as an epoll set, as on Linux.
The vector count is outside 0 through 1024, or a vector length is
negative as a signed size.
A native i686 write count is negative as the kernel's signed size after
its address range passes.
.Fn pwrite
was given a negative offset or an end past the signed 64-bit range,
computed with the original count before the transfer is limited.
A raw write has an offset or length not divisible by 512.
A device control write has an invalid argument or command.
.It Er EIO
A file system or device write failed, including a write to a protected stick.
A pseudoterminal slave has lost its master.
A background terminal write with
.Dv TOSTOP
also gives this error from an orphaned process group.
.It Er ELOOP
A loop-control attachment would exceed the permitted device nesting,
or its path has too many symbolic links.
.It Er EMSGSIZE
A datagram payload exceeds 65507 bytes over IPv4 or 65527 over IPv6.
.It Er ENAMETOOLONG
A path in a loop-control command exceeds a resolver limit.
.It Er ENETUNREACH
No interface has a route to the datagram destination, or a stream
connection timed out while its local address was gone.
.It Er ENOBUFS
The local datagram queue is full, or the network device cannot accept
the outgoing frame.
.It Er ENOENT
A loop-control command names an absent file or an unattached device.
.It Er ENOMEM
Resolving or opening a loop-control attachment requires unavailable memory.
.It Er ENOSPC
A file system has insufficient space,
a raw write starts at or crosses the end of its window, a framebuffer
write starts at the mode's end, or the descriptor refers to
.Pa /dev/full .
.It Er ENOTDIR
A component of a loop-control attachment path is not a directory.
.It Er ENXIO
A raw disk window is not present.
.It Er EOPNOTSUPP
The socket type does not support this write path.
.It Er EPERM
A whole-disk or partition node was written on the ordinary kernel,
a raw write was attempted without effective user ID 0,
a loop device node was written directly, the frame-allocation test
device was written without its boot option,
.Fn pwritev2
was given
.Dv RWF_NOAPPEND
on an append-only file, or a write to a memfd sealed with
.Dv F_SEAL_GROW
would pass its end with no byte of the write before the last 2 MiB
boundary at or below that end.
.It Er EPIPE
A pipe has no reader, or a stream socket can no longer send and has
no recorded error.
.It Er EROFS
The mount is read-only or its type has no write operation.
.It Er ESPIPE
.Fn pwrite
was given a descriptor that cannot seek.
.It Er ESRCH
There is no current process.
.It Er ETIMEDOUT
A stream connection timed out.
.El
